<article class="env-definition" id="06Z4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06Z4">Definition <span data-tag="06Z4">46.3.1</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring. A <em>module-valued functor</em> is a functor $F : \textit{Alg}_ A \to \textit{Ab}$ such that </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
for every object $B$ of $\textit{Alg}_ A$ the group $F(B)$ is endowed with the structure of a $B$-module, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for any morphism $B \to B'$ of $\textit{Alg}_ A$ the map $F(B) \to F(B')$ is $B$-linear. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> A <em>morphism of module-valued functors</em> is a transformation of functors $\varphi : F \to G$ such that $F(B) \to G(B)$ is $B$-linear for all $B \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\textit{Alg}_ A)$. </p>
</article>